Z||000000||Fformula88||02-09-2001||05:48 PM||||I've found another Fiero that I am considering getting, but just cannot seem to decide either way.-  Its an 87 GT, maroon with power everything, the factory subwoofer, an auto, spoiler etc etc.  I think the only thing not on the car is the sunroof.  Its got 94K miles on it, the interior is flawless (even the drivers seat is in great shape!) and seems to be running good.  He'll take $3000, and is asking $3500.<P>The dillema is that I would have to give up my 88 Formula to create the room (the 85 and 87 at the bottom are my fathers cars, but I list them 'cause we are living at the same household and I do a lot of work on them).  My Formula also has a few problems, most of them minor except the engine has never run right for me.  I am almost certain it has an 86 engine, and believe the problems I may be having are due to incompatability somewhere.  However, I have had it to tons of places with no success of a permanent repair.  Its not so bad that the car cannot be driven, but it is annoying.  It runs rough, esp. when cold and will stall out often.  Fires right back up and will usually work its way out of this.  The car has 80K on it, but I have no idea what the engine has.<P>I would love the GT.  But the Formula is my baby too, and I'd hate to loose it.  However, I am planning on keeping and preserving my Fiero for a long time, and since the Formula has mismatched parts I was thinking the GT may be a better investment.  Plus its suspension parts will be more readily available 5-10 years down the road than the 88's.<P>Thanks everyone.  Peace... <P>------------------<BR>John<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">      <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2m6<BR>1987 Fiero Sport Coupe *In rebuilding*<BR>1997 Ford Probe SE<BR>AOL IM: Fformula88||||reg||1
Z||000001||Neverfinished||02-09-2001||05:57 PM||||go for the GT!||||reg||1
Z||000002||2xsess||02-09-2001||06:23 PM||||The VIN is on the block, why don't you check it out and see where the engine came from?<P>I'd stick with the Formula, of course I'm kind of partial to notchbacks. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I've heard some people say that the 88s are going to be the more collectable ones, (this is not my opinion) <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">      <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||02-09-2001||06:31 PM||||Thats a good idea 2x, and I've tried to get to the engine number, but just cannot see it.  I've been above, under and around the engine with mirrors and flashlights, but cannot seem to find it.  I was told its supposed to be on the bell housing near the bottom, but I never could locate it.  It has the stickered intake plenum, not the engraved and it has the 85-86 oil pan.  All the heat shielding is also missing from the coil and alternator (and who knows where else.  <P>I agree that the 88's will be more collectable.  I even have a set of new front bearings that I am storing in case they become impossible to find.  They are nearly impossible to find new now.  However, I am not sure how collectable it will be with an incorrect engine in it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||2xsess||02-09-2001||09:19 PM||||From what I remember from seeing SMD1964s 87SE engine on the garage floor the number is on the upper part of the block , if your standing behind the car facing front it will be on the left side, not on the bellhousing though. I can't remember exactly where it is.<P>Well, it is your decision, no one can make it for you. My dad always used to say it's better to stick with the headaches you know rather than risk the ones you don't know, of course I never listened.hehe. <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">      <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
